Context, from the previous pages. m is an odd Gaussian prime and M is prime to m. The 'kleinste Reste' (least residues) of N mod m are taken as N - ωm, where ω = [N/m] is the componentwise floor. So the residue r satisfies r/m = x+iy with 0 ≤ x, y < 1. These residues fall into four quarter-squares: f (x<1/2, y<1/2), f' (x>1/2, y<1/2), f'' (both >1/2) and f''' (x<1/2, y>1/2). Since m is odd, x and y are fractions with odd denominator N(m), so x, y = 1/2 never occurs. One checks that f' ≡ i·f, f'' ≡ -f and f''' ≡ -i·f. The previous page proves the biquadratic Gauss lemma: if β, γ, δ of the products fM land in f', f'', f''', then M^{(N(m)-1)/4} ≡ i^{β+2γ+3δ} (mod m). The exponent ε is called the 'Decident' of M mod m.

(5, end) The class of the least residue of N can be read off from the integer part [2N/m] = ξ+ηi. We have 2N/m = 2ω + 2x + 2iy, so ξ ≡ ⌊2x⌋ and η ≡ ⌊2y⌋ (mod 2). Hence:
- f ⇔ ξ even, η even;
- f' ⇔ ξ odd, η even;
- f'' ⇔ ξ odd, η odd;
- f''' ⇔ ξ even, η odd.

(6) Hence a floor-function form of the lemma. For each f write [2fM/m] = x+yi and assign n = Θ(2fM/m) by parity: (even, even) → 0, (odd, even) → 1, (odd, odd) → 2, (even, odd) → 3. Then Dec(M/m) = ε ≡ Σ_f Θ(2fM/m) (mod 4). [My remark: for a, b ∈ {0,1} the parities of x, y, Θ ≡ a + 3b + 2ab mod 4.] This is the Z[i] analogue of the rational criterion (q/p) = (-1)^{Σ[2kq/p]}, k = 1, …, (p-1)/2.

(7) Now M is odd and primary, M ≡ 1 mod (2+2i). The f are split according to where their doubles fall: h with 2h ∈ f, h' with 2h' ∈ f', h'' with 2h'' ∈ f'', h''' with 2h''' ∈ f'''. The marginal note says, 'more briefly, the first class consists of the h whose double still lies in f'. Gauss writes ε = Σ over all four classes and then deletes it, since that is a tautology.

Key observation. Write 2h'/m = x+iy etc., with 0 < x, y < 1, since 2h/m ∈ [0,1)²:
- -i(2h'-m)/m = y + i(1-x);
- -(2h''-(1+i)m)/m = (1-x) + i(1-y);
- i(2h'''-im)/m = (1-y) + ix.
Each lies in the quarter-square f.

Mod 2 the four families 2h, -2h''+(1+i)m, -i(2h'-m), i(2h'''-im) are ≡ 0, (1+i)m, im, m respectively. These are four distinct classes of Z[i]/2 because m is odd [my justification of Gauss's 'sowohl unter sich als … verschieden']. So they are pairwise disjoint subsets of f, of total size |f|, and their union is f.

H = {2h} ∪ {(1+i)m - 2h''} is exactly the 'even' f, i.e. those divisible by 1+i. H' = {-i(2h'-m)} ∪ {i(2h'''-im)} is the odd f.

The page ends by announcing relations (worked out on the next page) that transform Θ(2h'M/m) etc. into ±Θ(·M/m) of elements of H, H'. They lead to ε ≡ Σ_H Θ(HM/m) - Σ_{H'} Θ(H'M/m): a biquadratic analogue of the 'Eisenstein-type' even-number form of Gauss's lemma, preparing the proof of biquadratic reciprocity.
